{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014WXKaViAVvkSYGw6rAj1Qd","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd010-ls-extended\n     2→title: \"cmd/ls: Extended Flags\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  prd008-ls covers the core flag subset for ls (default listing, -1, -l, -a, -A, -d, --color,\n     6→  -h). The extended flags add multi-column layout control (-C, -x), sort order options (-t,\n     7→  -S, -r, -U, -v), metadata display (-i, -s, -n), file type classification (-F), and\n     8→  recursive listing (-R). These flags require heavier interaction with pkg/format.Columns\n     9→  for layout control, pkg/sys.Stat for sort-by-metadata and inode/block display, and more\n    10→  complex output normalization in differential tests (inode numbers, block counts, and\n    11→  modification times all vary across test runs).\n    12→\n    13→  We extend prd008-ls here. All requirements in prd008-ls remain in force; we add the\n    14→  extended flag behavior without repeating core requirements.\n    15→\n    16→goals:\n    17→  - G1: Support multi-column layout control flags (-C, -x) for forced columnar output with vertical or horizontal sorting.\n    18→  - G2: Support sort order flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) to control entry ordering by time, size, version, or directory order.\n    19→  - G3: Support metadata display flags (-i, -s, -n) for inode numbers, block counts, and numeric user/group IDs.\n    20→  - G4: Support file type classification (-F) to append type indicators to entry names.\n    21→  - G5: Support recursive directory listing (-R) to list subdirectory contents.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Multi-column layout (-C, -x)\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: |\n    28→          -C must force multi-column output regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. Layout uses pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima. Terminal width is obtained via pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; returns error when stdout is not a terminal. When stdout is not a TTY, -C must use a default width of 80 columns (matching GNU ls behavior when stdout is not a TTY and -C is given without -w).\n    29→      - R1.2: -C must sort entries vertically (down columns, then across to the next column). This is the same sort direction as the default multi-column mode in prd008-ls R1.1.\n    30→      - R1.3: -x must produce multi-column output with entries sorted horizontally (across columns, then down to the next row). This differs from -C which sorts vertically.\n    31→      - R1.4: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. If -l or -1 appears after -C or -x on the command line, it overrides the multi-column mode. If -C or -x appears after -l or -1, it overrides the long or single-column format. The last format flag wins, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    32→\n    33→  R2:\n    34→    title: Sort order (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v)\n    35→    items:\n    36→      - R2.1: |\n    37→          -t must sort entries by modification time, newest first. Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error); use sys.FileInfo.ModTime time.Time for comparison (fi.ModTime.After(fj.ModTime) for newest-first ordering). Entries with the same modification time must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n    38→      - R2.2: |\n    39→          -S must sort entries by file size (st_size), largest first. Obtain file size from sys.FileInfo.Size int64 — apparent file size in bytes (st_size). Entries with the same size must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n    40→      - R2.3: -r must reverse the current sort order. -r applies to whichever sort is active (name sort by default, -t for time, -S for size, -v for version). -r with the default sort produces reverse alphabetical order.\n    41→      - R2.4: -U must disable sorting entirely and list entries in directory order (the order returned by readdir). -U overrides -t, -S, and -v. -r with -U has no defined effect (directory order is not reversible in a meaningful way), but must be accepted without error.\n    42→      - R2.5: -v must sort entries using natural version sort semantics (strverscmp behavior). Runs of digits within filenames are compared numerically rather than lexicographically, so \"file2\" sorts before \"file10\". Must be implemented without external libraries.\n    43→      - R2.6: When multiple sort flags are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    44→\n    45→  R3:\n    46→    title: Metadata display (-i, -s, -n)\n    47→    items:\n    48→      - R3.1: |\n    49→          -i must prepend the inode number of each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the inode number via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 — inode number (st_ino). The inode number is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest inode in the listing.\n    50→      - R3.2: |\n    51→          -s must prepend the allocated block count for each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the block count via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 — number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks). Report in 1024-byte block units: fi.Blocks / 2. The block count is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest count in the listing.\n    52→      - R3.3: -s with -l must also modify the \"total N\" line to include block counts in the selected unit. The total is the sum of all listed entries' block allocations.\n    53→      - R3.4: |\n    54→          -n must activate long format (implies -l) and display numeric UID and GID instead of resolving owner and group names. Format UID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10) and GID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10), where fi.Uid uint32 and fi.Gid uint32 come from pkg/sys.Lstat. Do not call os/user.LookupId or os/user.LookupGroupId.\n    55→      - R3.5: -i and -s may be combined. When both are given, inode is printed first, then block count, then the entry (or the long-format fields).\n    56→\n    57→  R4:\n    58→    title: File type classification (-F)\n    59→    items:\n    60→      - R4.1: -F must append a type indicator character after each entry name. The indicators are \"/\" for directories, \"*\" for executable regular files, \"@\" for symbolic links, \"|\" for named pipes (FIFOs), \"=\" for sockets. Regular non-executable files receive no indicator.\n    61→      - R4.2: Executable is defined as having any execute bit set (owner, group, or other) in the file's permission mode.\n    62→      - R4.3: -F must work with all output formats (-l, -1, -C, -x) and with color output. When color is enabled, the indicator character is printed after the ANSI reset sequence so it is not colorized.\n    63→\n    64→  R5:\n    65→    title: Recursive listing (-R)\n    66→    items:\n    67→      - R5.1: -R must recursively list the contents of each subdirectory encountered during the listing. Each subdirectory's contents are printed under a header line in the format \"PATH:\" followed by a blank line, then the directory contents, then a blank line before the next entry.\n    68→      - R5.2: -R must respect the current format mode. With -l, each subdirectory's contents are listed in long format including the \"total N\" block line. With -C or default mode, each subdirectory's contents use multi-column layout.\n    69→      - R5.3: -R must not follow symbolic links to directories. Only real subdirectories are recursed into.\n    70→      - R5.4: -R must apply the current filter flags (-a, -A) to each subdirectory. Dotfiles in subdirectories are shown only if -a or -A is given.\n    71→      - R5.5: -R must list directories in the same sort order as entries within a directory. If -t is active, subdirectories are recursed into in modification-time order.\n    72→\n    73→  R6:\n    74→    title: Flag interactions\n    75→    items:\n    76→      - R6.1: -n implies -l. When -n is given without -l, the output must be in long format with numeric UID/GID.\n    77→      - R6.2: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. The last format flag on the command line wins (see R1.4).\n    78→      - R6.3: -R with -l must produce a \"total N\" block line for each subdirectory listing, matching GNU ls -lR output.\n    79→      - R6.4: -i, -s, and -F may all be combined with -R. Each subdirectory listing applies the same metadata display and classification options.\n    80→      - R6.5: |\n    81→          -h must apply to -s block counts when both -s and -h are given, converting block counts to human-readable form. Use the following binary HumanSize algorithm directly in cmd/ls (do not call pkg/format.HumanSize):\n    82→\n    83→            func humanSize(n int64) string {\n    84→                if n == 0 { return \"0\" }\n    85→                suffixes := []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}\n    86→                f := float64(n)\n    87→                i := 0\n    88→                for f >= 1024 && i < len(suffixes)-1 { f /= 1024; i++ }\n    89→                if i == 0 { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d\", n) }\n    90→                if f == float64(int64(f)) { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d%s\", int64(f), suffixes[i]) }\n    91→                return fmt.Sprintf(\"%.1f%s\", f, suffixes[i])\n    92→            }\n    93→\n    94→non_goals:\n    95→  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) remains out of scope for this project.\n    96→  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) remains out of scope.\n    97→  - -Z (SELinux security context) remains out of scope.\n    98→  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) remains out of scope.\n    99→  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes remains out of scope.\n   100→  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing remains out of scope.\n   101→  - ACL display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) remains out of scope.\n   102→  - --group-directories-first is out of scope for this release.\n   103→  - -w / --width (explicit width override) is out of scope; terminal width is queried via pkg/sys.\n   104→  - --sort=WORD (long-form sort option) is out of scope; only short flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) are supported.\n   105→\n   106→acceptance_criteria:\n   107→  - \"AC1: Multi-column forced -- gls -C and go run ./cmd/ls -C produce identical column layout on a test fixture when both are given the same terminal width. Output must be piped (stdout not a TTY) to verify that -C forces multi-column even without a TTY.\"\n   108→  - \"AC2: Horizontal sort -- gls -x and go run ./cmd/ls -x produce identical output on a fixture. Entries must be arranged across rows first, then down.\"\n   109→  - \"AC3: Time sort -- gls -t and go run ./cmd/ls -t produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different modification times. Tests must create files with explicit mtimes to ensure deterministic ordering.\"\n   110→  - \"AC4: Size sort -- gls -S and go run ./cmd/ls -S produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different sizes.\"\n   111→  - \"AC5: Reverse sort -- gls -r and go run ./cmd/ls -r produce identical reverse-alphabetical output. gls -tr and the Go binary with -tr produce identical reverse-time output.\"\n   112→  - \"AC6: Inode display -- gls -i and go run ./cmd/ls -i produce identical output when run on the same live fixture on the same filesystem (inode numbers match because both binaries stat the same files).\"\n   113→  - \"AC7: Block count display -- gls -s and go run ./cmd/ls -s produce identical output. Differential tests must use the same filesystem to ensure block counts match.\"\n   114→  - \"AC8: Numeric IDs -- gls -n and go run ./cmd/ls -n produce identical long-format output with numeric UID and GID instead of user/group names.\"\n   115→  - \"AC9: Classify -- gls -F and go run ./cmd/ls -F produce identical output on a fixture containing a directory, a regular file, an executable, a symlink, and a named pipe.\"\n   116→  - \"AC10: Recursive listing -- gls -R and go run ./cmd/ls -R produce identical output on a fixture with nested subdirectories, including the \\\"path:\\\" headers and blank-line separators.\"\n   117→  - \"AC11: Version sort -- gls -v and go run ./cmd/ls -v produce identical output on a fixture containing files named file1, file2, file10, file20 (verifying numeric-aware sort).\"\n   118→  - \"AC12: cmd/ls with extended flags compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   119→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ab6c3f3-6c03-4d5b-9d8b-ea4468f3b690","uuid":"ad5be598-b66e-412a-a980-63deaf5c419f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/310/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd010-ls-extended.yaml","content":"id: prd010-ls-extended\ntitle: \"cmd/ls: Extended Flags\"\n\nproblem: |\n  prd008-ls covers the core flag subset for ls (default listing, -1, -l, -a, -A, -d, --color,\n  -h). The extended flags add multi-column layout control (-C, -x), sort order options (-t,\n  -S, -r, -U, -v), metadata display (-i, -s, -n), file type classification (-F), and\n  recursive listing (-R). These flags require heavier interaction with pkg/format.Columns\n  for layout control, pkg/sys.Stat for sort-by-metadata and inode/block display, and more\n  complex output normalization in differential tests (inode numbers, block counts, and\n  modification times all vary across test runs).\n\n  We extend prd008-ls here. All requirements in prd008-ls remain in force; we add the\n  extended flag behavior without repeating core requirements.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Support multi-column layout control flags (-C, -x) for forced columnar output with vertical or horizontal sorting.\n  - G2: Support sort order flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) to control entry ordering by time, size, version, or directory order.\n  - G3: Support metadata display flags (-i, -s, -n) for inode numbers, block counts, and numeric user/group IDs.\n  - G4: Support file type classification (-F) to append type indicators to entry names.\n  - G5: Support recursive directory listing (-R) to list subdirectory contents.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Multi-column layout (-C, -x)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: |\n          -C must force multi-column output regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. Layout uses pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima. Terminal width is obtained via pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; returns error when stdout is not a terminal. When stdout is not a TTY, -C must use a default width of 80 columns (matching GNU ls behavior when stdout is not a TTY and -C is given without -w).\n      - R1.2: -C must sort entries vertically (down columns, then across to the next column). This is the same sort direction as the default multi-column mode in prd008-ls R1.1.\n      - R1.3: -x must produce multi-column output with entries sorted horizontally (across columns, then down to the next row). This differs from -C which sorts vertically.\n      - R1.4: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. If -l or -1 appears after -C or -x on the command line, it overrides the multi-column mode. If -C or -x appears after -l or -1, it overrides the long or single-column format. The last format flag wins, matching GNU ls behavior.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Sort order (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          -t must sort entries by modification time, newest first. Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error); use sys.FileInfo.ModTime time.Time for comparison (fi.ModTime.After(fj.ModTime) for newest-first ordering). Entries with the same modification time must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n      - R2.2: |\n          -S must sort entries by file size (st_size), largest first. Obtain file size from sys.FileInfo.Size int64 — apparent file size in bytes (st_size). Entries with the same size must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n      - R2.3: -r must reverse the current sort order. -r applies to whichever sort is active (name sort by default, -t for time, -S for size, -v for version). -r with the default sort produces reverse alphabetical order.\n      - R2.4: -U must disable sorting entirely and list entries in directory order (the order returned by readdir). -U overrides -t, -S, and -v. -r with -U has no defined effect (directory order is not reversible in a meaningful way), but must be accepted without error.\n      - R2.5: -v must sort entries using natural version sort semantics (strverscmp behavior). Runs of digits within filenames are compared numerically rather than lexicographically, so \"file2\" sorts before \"file10\". Must be implemented without external libraries.\n      - R2.6: When multiple sort flags are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Metadata display (-i, -s, -n)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          -i must prepend the inode number of each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the inode number via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 — inode number (st_ino). The inode number is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest inode in the listing.\n      - R3.2: |\n          -s must prepend the allocated block count for each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the block count via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 — number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks). Report in 1024-byte block units: fi.Blocks / 2. The block count is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest count in the listing.\n      - R3.3: -s with -l must also modify the \"total N\" line to include block counts in the selected unit. The total is the sum of all listed entries' block allocations.\n      - R3.4: |\n          -n must activate long format (implies -l) and display numeric UID and GID instead of resolving owner and group names. Format UID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10) and GID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10), where fi.Uid uint32 and fi.Gid uint32 come from pkg/sys.Lstat. Do not call os/user.LookupId or os/user.LookupGroupId.\n      - R3.5: -i and -s may be combined. When both are given, inode is printed first, then block count, then the entry (or the long-format fields).\n\n  R4:\n    title: File type classification (-F)\n    items:\n      - R4.1: -F must append a type indicator character after each entry name. The indicators are \"/\" for directories, \"*\" for executable regular files, \"@\" for symbolic links, \"|\" for named pipes (FIFOs), \"=\" for sockets. Regular non-executable files receive no indicator.\n      - R4.2: Executable is defined as having any execute bit set (owner, group, or other) in the file's permission mode.\n      - R4.3: -F must work with all output formats (-l, -1, -C, -x) and with color output. When color is enabled, the indicator character is printed after the ANSI reset sequence so it is not colorized.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Recursive listing (-R)\n    items:\n      - R5.1: -R must recursively list the contents of each subdirectory encountered during the listing. Each subdirectory's contents are printed under a header line in the format \"PATH:\" followed by a blank line, then the directory contents, then a blank line before the next entry.\n      - R5.2: -R must respect the current format mode. With -l, each subdirectory's contents are listed in long format including the \"total N\" block line. With -C or default mode, each subdirectory's contents use multi-column layout.\n      - R5.3: -R must not follow symbolic links to directories. Only real subdirectories are recursed into.\n      - R5.4: -R must apply the current filter flags (-a, -A) to each subdirectory. Dotfiles in subdirectories are shown only if -a or -A is given.\n      - R5.5: -R must list directories in the same sort order as entries within a directory. If -t is active, subdirectories are recursed into in modification-time order.\n\n  R6:\n    title: Flag interactions\n    items:\n      - R6.1: -n implies -l. When -n is given without -l, the output must be in long format with numeric UID/GID.\n      - R6.2: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. The last format flag on the command line wins (see R1.4).\n      - R6.3: -R with -l must produce a \"total N\" block line for each subdirectory listing, matching GNU ls -lR output.\n      - R6.4: -i, -s, and -F may all be combined with -R. Each subdirectory listing applies the same metadata display and classification options.\n      - R6.5: |\n          -h must apply to -s block counts when both -s and -h are given, converting block counts to human-readable form. Use the following binary HumanSize algorithm directly in cmd/ls (do not call pkg/format.HumanSize):\n\n            func humanSize(n int64) string {\n                if n == 0 { return \"0\" }\n                suffixes := []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}\n                f := float64(n)\n                i := 0\n                for f >= 1024 && i < len(suffixes)-1 { f /= 1024; i++ }\n                if i == 0 { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d\", n) }\n                if f == float64(int64(f)) { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d%s\", int64(f), suffixes[i]) }\n                return fmt.Sprintf(\"%.1f%s\", f, suffixes[i])\n            }\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) remains out of scope for this project.\n  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) remains out of scope.\n  - -Z (SELinux security context) remains out of scope.\n  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) remains out of scope.\n  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes remains out of scope.\n  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing remains out of scope.\n  - ACL display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) remains out of scope.\n  - --group-directories-first is out of scope for this release.\n  - -w / --width (explicit width override) is out of scope; terminal width is queried via pkg/sys.\n  - --sort=WORD (long-form sort option) is out of scope; only short flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) are supported.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Multi-column forced -- gls -C and go run ./cmd/ls -C produce identical column layout on a test fixture when both are given the same terminal width. Output must be piped (stdout not a TTY) to verify that -C forces multi-column even without a TTY.\"\n  - \"AC2: Horizontal sort -- gls -x and go run ./cmd/ls -x produce identical output on a fixture. Entries must be arranged across rows first, then down.\"\n  - \"AC3: Time sort -- gls -t and go run ./cmd/ls -t produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different modification times. Tests must create files with explicit mtimes to ensure deterministic ordering.\"\n  - \"AC4: Size sort -- gls -S and go run ./cmd/ls -S produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different sizes.\"\n  - \"AC5: Reverse sort -- gls -r and go run ./cmd/ls -r produce identical reverse-alphabetical output. gls -tr and the Go binary with -tr produce identical reverse-time output.\"\n  - \"AC6: Inode display -- gls -i and go run ./cmd/ls -i produce identical output when run on the same live fixture on the same filesystem (inode numbers match because both binaries stat the same files).\"\n  - \"AC7: Block count display -- gls -s and go run ./cmd/ls -s produce identical output. Differential tests must use the same filesystem to ensure block counts match.\"\n  - \"AC8: Numeric IDs -- gls -n and go run ./cmd/ls -n produce identical long-format output with numeric UID and GID instead of user/group names.\"\n  - \"AC9: Classify -- gls -F and go run ./cmd/ls -F produce identical output on a fixture containing a directory, a regular file, an executable, a symlink, and a named pipe.\"\n  - \"AC10: Recursive listing -- gls -R and go run ./cmd/ls -R produce identical output on a fixture with nested subdirectories, including the \\\"path:\\\" headers and blank-line separators.\"\n  - \"AC11: Version sort -- gls -v and go run ./cmd/ls -v produce identical output on a fixture containing files named file1, file2, file10, file20 (verifying numeric-aware sort).\"\n  - \"AC12: cmd/ls with extended flags compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}}}
