cope, so I'll stick with just `--sort=none` as an alias for `-U` and reject any other `--sort` values with an error. Handling the --sort flag pragmatically...\n\nI'll implement support for --sort=none specifically and return an error for any other --sort values, directing users to use the short flags instead. This keeps the implementation simple while staying true to the PRD's scope. Writing the flag parsing logic... Handling the long flags with a switch statement...\n\nI'm refining the --sort flag parsing to validate the value and provide a clearer error message when an invalid sort option is passed. Now I need to write out the full implementation and also figure out the correct time format strings that match GNU ls behavior, particularly for full-iso and long-iso formats with their specific date and time components. I'm double-checking the exact formatting that GNU ls uses for these timestamp fields—whether the old ISO format includes a trailing space for padding and how the character widths actually line up across recent versus older files.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the locale format under LC_ALL=C, which uses a different pattern with abbreviated month names and right-aligned day numbers, both for recent and old timestamps. The default behavior without any time-style flag matches this locale format.\n\nWriting the formatTime function... Writing the time formatting logic...\n\nI'm second-guessing the trailing space in the ISO old format—it might cause issues with string trimming later, so I'll leave it out for now and handle alignment separately if needed when implementing the -l mode. Let me also double-check the FileInfo struct definition in the existing code before I start writing.\n\nI need to add AccessTime and ChangeTime fields to the struct after ModTime, then plan out my edits in order: first the pkg/sys changes across three files, then the new sort.go file, then the main.go modifications, and finally verify everything builds and passes vet.","signature":""}]